"
\h0#preface|Preface|

This document describes the IW4x Steam companion (\c{companion}), a Steam
client module that shows IW4x to Steam friends as the game being played,
along with \c{libcompanion}, the library it is built from. For the command
line interface of the Linux launcher refer to the \l{iw4x-steam(1)} man
page.

\h1#intro|Introduction|

Steam tells each of our friends which game we are playing, and the CM server
(the Steam server that the client stays connected to) only shows a game that
our account owns. IW4x is not a Steam game, so friends do not see it.

The companion solves this from inside the Steam client. The client reports
the games that are running to the CM server with the \c{GamesPlayed}
message, one entry per game. The companion intercepts this message on its
way out and, in each entry that refers to a running IW4x process, replaces
the game with one that the account owns, Call of Duty: Modern Warfare 2
(app 10190) or, if the account does not own it, Spacewar (app 480), which
every account owns. It also sets the title that friends see to the one the
game provides, for example, \c{IW4x: Modern Warfare 2}. Every other message
is sent unchanged.

For this to work, each side needs to know about the other. The game
registers itself with the companion by publishing a small record with the
app id and the title. The companion, in turn, publishes its status, which
tells the game whether the companion is running and whether it managed to
hook the Steam client. We call this exchange the game protocol and describe
it in \l{#protocol Game Protocol}.

The companion exists in two builds, one for each Steam client that IW4x
runs with:

\dl|

\li|\c{\b{iw4x-steam64.dll}}

The Windows module for the 64-bit Windows Steam client. The game ships it
next to \c{iw4x.dll} and loads it into the running Steam client, so there
is nothing to install.|

\li|\c{\b{libiw4x-steam.so}}

The Linux module for the 32-bit native Linux Steam client, which runs the
game under Proton. The Steam client can only load it at startup, so it is
installed together with the \c{iw4x-steam} launcher, which starts the
Steam client with the module preloaded.||

Both modules are built from \c{libcompanion}, which contains the
platform-independent parts of the companion: the parsing of the Steam
client image, the location of the function to hook, the decoding of the
records, and the rewriting of the reports. Because it has no dependency on
either operating system, it is built and tested on any host.

We begin with a walkthrough that installs the Linux companion and then look
at how the companion works, at each of the two platforms, and at the game
protocol in more detail.

\h1#start|Getting Started|

On Windows there is nothing to do: the game loads the companion into the
Steam client every time it starts. So in this chapter we set up the Linux
companion.

The Linux module requires glibc 2.36 or later (Debian 12, for example), and
building it requires GCC 16 or later together with the 32-bit glibc and
libstdc++ development files. On Fedora, for example, these are the
\c{glibc-devel.i686} and \c{libstdc++-devel.i686} packages.

The Steam client is a 32-bit program and the programs that start it are
64-bit. This is why the companion is installed from two build
configurations into the same location: an i386 configuration that provides
the module and the launcher, and an x86_64 configuration that provides the
empty 64-bit stand-ins of the module. We will see what the stand-ins are for
in \l{#linux-preload Preloading}.

First, we create the two configurations, specifying the installation
location. The installation filter limits the installation to what the
Steam client needs, leaving out the library's headers and \c{pkg-config}
files:

\
$ f='lib/iw4x-steam/@true lib/@false include/@false'

$ bpkg create -d companion-i686 cc   \\
  config.cxx='g++ -m32'            \\
  config.cxx.target=i686-linux-gnu \\
  config.c='gcc -m32'              \\
  config.c.target=i686-linux-gnu   \\
  config.cc.coptions=-O2           \\
  config.install.root=/usr/local   \\
  config.install.sudo=sudo         \\
  config.install.filter=\"$f\"

$ bpkg create -d companion-x86_64 cc \\
  config.cxx=g++                   \\
  config.cc.coptions=-O2           \\
  config.install.root=/usr/local   \\
  config.install.sudo=sudo         \\
  config.install.filter=\"$f\"
\

Note that GCC only reports the i386 target that \c{-m32} selects if it is
built with multiarch support (Debian, Ubuntu), which is why we specify the
target explicitly.

Then we build and install the companion in each configuration:

\
$ bpkg build -d companion-i686 \\
  libcompanion@https://github.com/iw4x/companion.git#main
$ bpkg install -d companion-i686 libcompanion

$ bpkg build -d companion-x86_64 \\
  libcompanion@https://github.com/iw4x/companion.git#main
$ bpkg install -d companion-x86_64 libcompanion
\

Now we can start Steam with the companion. If Steam is already running, we
exit it first, since the module can only be loaded when the client starts:

\
$ iw4x-steam
\

The launcher passes its arguments to \c{steam} and refuses to start if
Steam is still running. See \l{iw4x-steam(1)} for details.

The companion writes its diagnostics to the Steam client's \c{stderr},
which the client copies to its console log. Let's see what it has to say:

\
$ grep -o '\\[iw4x-steam\\].*' ~/.steam/steam/logs/console-linux.txt
[iw4x-steam] send function at steamclient.so+0x2dca890 (1 references)
[iw4x-steam] hooked the send function (9 bytes relocated)
[iw4x-steam] active (build 2)
\

The first line tells us that the companion found the function through
which the Steam client sends its messages, at this offset in
\c{steamclient.so}. The second line says that it hooked this function and
the last one that the companion is active. From this point on it is idle
until a game registers.

If we now start the game and look at the log again, we see two more lines:

\
[iw4x-steam] record changed
[iw4x-steam] resent GamesPlayed on connection 0xdced7cd0 (294 -> 308
  bytes, after a frame on 0xdce81d40)
\

Steam reported the game right after starting it, before the game had a
chance to register. So once the game published its record, the companion
rewrote the last report and sent it again. From now on our friends see
IW4x as the game we are playing.

\h1#operation|How the Companion Works|

In this chapter we look at what the companion does inside the Steam
client, which is the same on both platforms. The platform-specific parts,
namely, how the companion gets into the Steam client and how it finds the
game's record, are the subject of the next two chapters.

\h#operation-startup|Startup|

Once loaded into the Steam client, the companion performs the following
steps on its own thread:

\ol|

\li|Publish its status with the \c{starting} state. Publishing the status
also makes sure there is a single companion, so a second copy of the module
finds the status already published and does nothing.|

\li|Find the send function in the Steam client library,
\c{steamclient64.dll} on Windows and \c{steamclient.so} on Linux.|

\li|Hook the send function.|

\li|Start watching for changes to the records.|

\li|Publish the \c{active} state.||

If the companion does not recognize the Steam client library, then it
publishes the \c{unsupported} state. And if it fails to hook the function,
then it publishes the \c{failed} state. In both cases it does nothing else
and the Steam client continues as if the module was never loaded.

\h#operation-locate|Locating the Send Function|

The Steam client sends every message to the CM server through the
\c{CWebSocketConnection::BBuildAndAsyncSendFrame()} function. The Steam
client library does not export it and its machine code changes with almost
every Steam build, so the companion cannot find it by a byte signature.
It finds the function by its structure.

The function opens a profiler scope named after itself. As a result, the
library contains the function name as a string, which we call the anchor,
and the function loads the anchor's address. The companion parses the
library image, finds every instruction that loads the anchor's address, and
maps each of them to the function that contains it using the unwind
information. How the send function is then picked among these functions
depends on the architecture. On x64 the functions that inline the send
function also load the anchor, and the send function is the one that loads
it within 256 bytes of its entry, right after its prologue. On i386 the
address is computed relative to the global offset table, and the send
function is the one that loads the table's address itself.

The search must end with exactly one function. Any other result means a
Steam build that the companion does not recognize, which it reports as
\c{unsupported}. The diagnostics include the number of references and
candidates that were found, for example:

\
[iw4x-steam] unable to locate the send function: ambiguous (4
  references, 2 candidates)
\

\h#operation-hook|The Send Hook|

The companion hooks the send function with a detour: it overwrites the
start of the function with a jump to its own code, which calls the original
function through a trampoline. On Windows the detour is installed with
MinHook. On Linux, where the Steam client is 32-bit, the companion builds
its own i386 detour and refuses to install it if the overwritten
instructions contain anything it cannot relocate.

The hook runs on the Steam client's network thread for every message that
the client sends. It recognizes a \c{GamesPlayed} message from its header,
which only takes a few comparisons, and passes every other message to the
original function unchanged.

\h#operation-rewrite|Rewriting Reports|

A \c{GamesPlayed} report contains a \c{GamePlayed} entry for each running
game, which includes the id of the game and the id of the process that
Steam tracks for it. For each entry the companion looks for a record that
the game in this process has published (see \l{#protocol Game Protocol}).
If there is one, then the companion sets the game id to the app id from the
record and the game's extra information, which the CM server shows to
friends as the title, to the title from the record.

The rest of the report is copied byte for byte. A report that contains no
entries of registered processes is sent unchanged, and so is a report
larger than 8192 bytes, which is far beyond a realistic report.

\h#operation-resend|Resending Reports|

Steam reports a game right after starting it, normally before the game has
published its record. After that it only sends a new report when the set of
running games changes. The game can also update its record while it is
running, for example, to switch from Modern Warfare 2 to Spacewar. So
without further measures, friends would not see IW4x until something else
changes.

To handle this, the companion keeps the last report along with the message
that was actually sent for it, and watches the records for changes. On a
change it rewrites the report again and, if the result differs from what
the CM server last received, sends it on the same connection.

The companion has no thread of its own in the Steam client's networking
code, so the resend happens when the Steam client sends the next message on
the thread that sent the last report. Steam uses a single thread per
connection, so the resend never interleaves with the client's own messages
on this connection. If the connection has been idle for 60 seconds, then
the companion assumes it is closed and drops the report.

\h1#linux|The Linux Companion|

The native Linux Steam client runs Windows games with Proton, in a
container provided by the Steam Runtime. In this chapter we look at how the
companion gets into the Steam client and how it finds the record of a game
that runs in such a container.

\h#linux-preload|Preloading|

The Steam client does not load plugins, so the module is loaded with
\c{LD_PRELOAD}. For this reason it must be in place when the Steam client
starts and the \c{iw4x-steam} launcher takes care of this.

The Steam client is started by a chain of 64-bit programs, which all
inherit \c{LD_PRELOAD}. A
64-bit program cannot load the 32-bit module and its dynamic linker warns
about each program that it fails to preload. To avoid this, the launcher
names the module with the dynamic linker's \c{$PLATFORM} in its path:

\
LD_PRELOAD=/usr/local/lib/iw4x-steam/$PLATFORM/libiw4x-steam.so
\

The dynamic linker expands \c{$PLATFORM} separately in each process. In the
32-bit Steam client it expands to \c{i686}, which contains the module. In
the 64-bit programs it expands to \c{x86_64} or, depending on the CPU, to
\c{haswell} or \c{xeon_phi}, each of which contains an empty 64-bit
stand-in.

In every process that it is loaded into, the module first removes itself
from \c{LD_PRELOAD}, so the processes that this one starts in turn, such as
Proton and the games, do not load it. In any process other than the Steam
client, identified by its executable name, \c{steam}, this is all the
module does.

\h#linux-startup|Startup|

The companion publishes its status in the
\c{/dev/shm/iw4x-steam-companion.\i{uid}} file, where \i{uid} is the user
id, and keeps the file locked while it runs. A second companion of the same
user, for example, one in a second Steam client, fails to lock the file and
does nothing:

\
[iw4x-steam] another companion is resident, staying inert
\

The Steam client loads \c{steamclient.so} after its bootstrapper finishes
updating it, which can take a while on a slow connection. So the companion
waits up to 30 minutes for the library to load before it gives up and
publishes the \c{unsupported} state.

When the Steam client exits, the companion removes the status file. If the
client crashes, then the file is left behind and the next companion takes
it over. Meanwhile, the game recognizes the status as stale by the Steam
client's process id that it contains.

\h#linux-lookup|Finding the Game|

The native Steam client cannot see the Windows objects of a game running
under Wine, so the game publishes its record as a file in \c{/dev/shm},
which the Proton container shares with the host. The question is which
file belongs to which report.

Steam reports a game with the process id of the process it tracks for it,
which is one of Steam's own helpers or Proton's processes. The game itself
cannot find out its Linux process id, since Wine performs its file
operations in the \c{wineserver} process of its prefix. So the
game names its record after the process id of this \c{wineserver}, which
Proton starts in the process tree that Steam tracks.

To find the record of a report, the companion searches the reported process
and its descendants, breadth-first. For each process it tries the record
under each of the process's ids, one per process id namespace that the
process is in, since the game sees the ids of the container. The record
also contains the start time of the \c{wineserver} process, which tells a
record apart from a stale one left by an earlier process with the same id.

A game under Proton consists of a few dozen processes, so the search visits
at most 256 and issues diagnostics if it runs out of processes to visit:

\
[iw4x-steam] no record for process 4242 (overflow)
\

It also issues diagnostics if the reported process is the Steam client or
one of its ancestors (\c{related}), whose tree contains every game and is
therefore not searched.

\h#linux-glibc|glibc Compatibility|

The module runs in the Steam client and so with the glibc of the system.
It links
the C++ runtime statically and binds its glibc references to versions no
newer than 2.36. As a result, one build runs on any distribution with
glibc 2.36 or later, which the module's tests verify.

\h1#windows|The Windows Companion|

On Windows the Steam client is a 64-bit program and the game is a 32-bit
one. A 32-bit process cannot load a module into a 64-bit one, so the game
enlists the help of the 64-bit \c{rundll32.exe}.

\h#windows-attach|Attaching|

The game attaches the companion to the Steam client by running the
\c{IW4xAttach} entry point of the module in the 64-bit \c{rundll32.exe},
passing the Steam client's process id:

\
rundll32.exe \"...\\iw4x-steam64.dll\",IW4xAttach 5120
\

The entry point verifies that the process is a 64-bit Steam client and
loads the module into it by calling \c{LoadLibraryW()} on a remote thread.
It then waits for the companion to settle and returns the result as the
\c{rundll32.exe} exit code:

\dl|

\li|\c{\b{0} active}

The companion is active.|

\li|\c{\b{1} unsupported}

The companion does not recognize the Steam client library.|

\li|\c{\b{2} failed}

The companion failed to hook the send function.|

\li|\c{\b{3} invalid_arguments}

The command line does not contain a valid process id.|

\li|\c{\b{4} not_steam}

The process is not a 64-bit Steam client.|

\li|\c{\b{5} access_denied}

The process cannot be opened, for example, because it runs with higher
privileges.|

\li|\c{\b{6} injection_failed}

The module could not be loaded into the process.|

\li|\c{\b{7} timeout}

The module was not loaded within 10 seconds or the companion did not settle
within 5 seconds.||

If the companion is already running in the Steam client, for example, from
an earlier game session, then the entry point reports its state without
loading anything.

\h#windows-startup|Startup|

The companion publishes its status in a named file mapping whose name
includes the Steam client's process id. Creating the mapping is also what
makes sure there is a single companion per Steam client. It then finds the
send function in \c{steamclient64.dll}, which the Steam client loads before
any game can start (if it is not loaded, then the companion reports the
\c{unsupported} state), and proceeds as described in \l{#operation-startup
Startup}. The module stays loaded until the Steam client exits since, once
the hook is installed, the client executes its code.

The game publishes its record in a named file mapping of its own and, after
every change to the record, signals a named event, which is how the
companion learns about the change.

The companion writes its diagnostics to the debugger output, which can be
viewed with a tool such as DebugView. Each line starts with
\c{[iw4x-steam64]}.

\h1#protocol|Game Protocol|

The game protocol is how the game and the companion communicate. It is
defined in \c{<libcompanion/protocol.hxx>}, which only depends on the
standard library, so that the game can include it directly.

\h#protocol-compat|Compatibility|

The game, the Windows companion, and the Linux companion are each built
with their own toolchain for their own architecture and each is upgraded on
its own schedule. The companion also stays loaded until the Steam client
exits, so any version of the game can encounter any version of the
companion.

For this reason every structure of the protocol has a fixed layout of
32-bit fields and starts with a magic, a version, and its size. A reader
accepts only the exact layout it knows and every change to a layout
increments its version. In addition, the companion publishes its build
number, which is incremented with every change of the companion's behavior
that the game can observe. The current build is 2.

\h#protocol-record|Registration Record|

The game registers itself by publishing a record with the app id to report
and the title to show:

\
struct record
{
  uint32_t magic;          // 0x50345749 (\"IW4P\")
  uint32_t version;        // 1
  uint32_t size;           // 84
  uint32_t process_id;     // Game process id.
  uint32_t app_id;
  char     extra_info[64]; // Title, UTF-8, NUL-terminated.
};
\

The app id must be non-zero and fit into 24 bits. The title must be
non-empty, fit into the 64 bytes with the terminating \c{NUL}, be valid
UTF-8, and contain no control characters. The game can update both of them
while it runs. For example, it starts with Modern Warfare 2 (app 10190)
and switches to Spacewar (app 480) if Steam reports that the account does
not own it.

On Windows the record is published in a named file mapping,
\c{Local\\IW4x.SteamPresence.}\i{pid}, where \i{pid} is the game's process
id, which the record repeats. The mapping lives while the game keeps a
handle to it, so the record goes away with the game. The game writes the
magic last with release ordering and the companion reads it first with
acquire ordering, so a record with the right magic is always complete.
After every change to the record, the game sets the auto-reset event
\c{Local\\IW4x.SteamPresence.Changed.}\i{steam-pid}, where
\i{steam-pid} is the Steam client's process id.

On Linux the record is published in the \c{/dev/shm/iw4x-presence.}\i{pid}
file, where \i{pid} is the process id of the game's \c{wineserver} (see
\l{#linux-lookup Finding the Game}). The game opens it through Wine's
\c{\\\\?\\unix\\} path prefix and writes it in one go. The record has two
more fields, the \c{wineserver} start time from field 22 of
\c{/proc/}\i{pid}\c{/stat}:

\
struct unix_record
{
  uint32_t magic;           // 0x55345749 (\"IW4U\")
  uint32_t version;         // 1
  uint32_t size;            // 92
  uint32_t process_id;      // wineserver process id.
  uint32_t start_time_low;  // wineserver start time.
  uint32_t start_time_high;
  uint32_t app_id;
  char     extra_info[64];
};
\

\h#protocol-status|Companion Status|

The companion publishes its status, which the game reads to find out whether
the companion is running and in which state. The status contains the
companion's state:

\dl|

\li|\c{\b{0} starting}

The companion is initializing.|

\li|\c{\b{1} active}

The send hook is installed and the records are applied.|

\li|\c{\b{2} unsupported}

The companion does not recognize the Steam client library.|

\li|\c{\b{3} failed}

The companion failed to hook the send function.||

On Windows the status is published in a named file mapping,
\c{Local\\IW4x.SteamCompanion.}\i{steam-pid}, where \i{steam-pid} is the
Steam client's process id. The companion stores the state last with
release ordering and the game loads it first with acquire ordering:

\
struct status
{
  uint32_t magic;   // 0x53345749 (\"IW4S\")
  uint32_t version; // 1
  uint32_t size;    // 24
  uint32_t state;
  uint32_t build;   // Companion build.
  uint32_t hook;    // Send function offset or 0.
};
\

On Linux the status is published in the
\c{/dev/shm/iw4x-steam-companion.}\i{uid} file, where \i{uid} is the real
user id, which the game reads from \c{/proc/self/status}. It also contains
the Steam client's process id, which allows the game to recognize a status
left behind by a crashed client:

\
struct unix_status
{
  uint32_t magic;     // 0x54345749 (\"IW4T\")
  uint32_t version;   // 1
  uint32_t size;      // 28
  uint32_t state;
  uint32_t build;
  uint32_t hook;
  uint32_t steam_pid; // Steam client process id.
};
\

\h1#usage|Usage|

The \c{libcompanion} package is available from its git repository. To
install the Linux companion, follow the steps in \l{#start Getting
Started}.

To start using \c{libcompanion} in your project, add the following
\c{depends} value to your \c{manifest}, adjusting the version constraint
as appropriate:

\
depends: libcompanion ^0.1.0
\

Then import the library in your \c{buildfile}:

\
import libs = libcompanion%lib{companion}
\

Note that the library interface is internal to the companion and is not
stable. The game protocol (\c{<libcompanion/protocol.hxx>}) is the
exception and follows the compatibility rules described in
\l{#protocol-compat Compatibility}.

The package provides the following importable targets:

\
lib{companion}
libs{iw4x-steam64}
libs{iw4x-steam}
exe{iw4x-steam}
\

The \c{lib{companion\}} library contains the platform-independent parts of
the companion. The \c{libs{iw4x-steam64\}} target is the Windows module and
is only built for x86_64 Windows. The \c{libs{iw4x-steam\}} target is the
Linux module and \c{exe{iw4x-steam\}} is its launcher. Both are only built
for i386 Linux and the launcher only when installing.
"
